WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:03.103
First of all, congratulations.

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:05.472
I saw the movie last week.

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:08.541
It is an astonishing piece

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:11.544
of art and technical wizardry.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:12.612
Thank you. That's very.

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:13.446
That's very kind.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:13.680
Yeah.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.048
People seem to be connecting with it,

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:16.850
which is all we could have hoped for.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.386
13 years after the first avatar

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:20.820
and five years

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.556
after starting production on this one.

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:25.825
How do you feel about what you're now

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:27.394
sharing with the world, Jim?

00:00:27.394 --> 00:00:29.529
Are you satisfied with it?

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:29.829
Yeah.

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:31.564
I was satisfied before we put it out.

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.299
But that doesn't necessarily mean

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:35.001
that people

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:36.202
people are going to be interested.

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:36.903
You never know.

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:38.705
I mean, it's just the nature of our

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:39.572
our business.

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:40.440
That you never really know

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.641
until you put something out there

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:42.442
in the zeitgeist

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:43.676
if people are going to respond.

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:44.244
But it

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.413
it seems to be the right film

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:47.080
at the right time.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:47.547
And I don't know

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:47.914
if that's

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:49.349
that sense of childlike

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:51.885
wonder that can engender in an audience.

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:54.854
Or maybe it's the emotional processing

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.290
that they go through when they watch it.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:58.091
Almost everybody

00:00:58.091 --> 00:00:58.558
that sees

00:00:58.558 --> 00:00:59.826
it tells me that they

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:02.262
they cry or they tear up at some point.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.064
So I think it's working on

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:05.632
on multiple levels.

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:06.766
So that's very satisfying.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.301
I mean, I'm just sort of reeling

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:10.203
from the fact that I'm unemployed

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:11.938
for the first time in five years.

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:15.542
Let's talk about that for a minute.

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:16.409
You know,

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:17.343
you've obviously

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:18.812
had an extraordinary

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:20.480
history of successes,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.982
but as you say, nobody knows.

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:23.983
We are nervous

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:25.919
when finally you had to share this

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:27.053
with the world.

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:27.687
Yeah, very.

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:28.488
I think you're all

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:29.522
I think any filmmaker

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.257
that says they're not nervous before

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.326
their film drops is is you know,

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:35.995
lying or or, you know, in denial.

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:37.997
Yeah. You of course you're nervous.

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:38.231
You know,

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:38.832
the zeitgeist

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:40.667
changes, people's tastes change.

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.001
We're in a.

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:43.436
Coming out of a COVID era,

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:45.371
but we're still not at full strength

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:48.808
in the, you know, theatrical community.

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:50.743
We're only running at about maybe

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:53.880
65 to 75% of what we used to be.

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:56.716
So we're trying to build that back.

00:01:57.584 --> 00:01:57.817
Yeah.

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:58.284
So there's

00:01:58.284 --> 00:01:59.419
there were a lot of variables,

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:00.420
a lot of factors

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.623
that made it quite daunting You say this

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:06.359
avatar is more emotional

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:07.794
than the first one.

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:08.728
And part of that

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:11.397
is that you have given Sully and Neytiri

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:14.000
a sprawling family of children.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:16.336
Why did you decide to go that route?

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:17.504
Well, I think it's a couple of things.

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:18.571
I mean, one, you know,

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:19.873
they say write what you know.

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:22.142
And as a as a family man,

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:23.309
as a father of five,

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:23.910
this is something

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.512
that I'd never really dealt with that

00:02:25.512 --> 00:02:26.779
I wanted to deal with.

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:27.814
I also wanted something

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.816
that would have a universal appeal

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.451
for people in any culture

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:32.118
or language

00:02:32.118 --> 00:02:33.019
or religion

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:33.753
around the world,

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:35.221
because the Avatar audience

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:36.222
is a global audience,

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:36.556
which we're

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:39.559
seeing again on on this release.

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:40.927
And I just

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:41.928
I wanted the characters

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:43.730
to have stakes, like real stakes.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.165
I wanted to deal with

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:48.201
with as much as the beauty and wonder.

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:48.768
On the one

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:49.068
hand,

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:51.504
I also wanted to do with something, deal

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:51.905
with something

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:52.739
deeper, you know,

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:54.207
which is like grief and loss

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:57.143
and how we process these deep bonds

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:59.345
between between each other.

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.114
One of the ways that you show

00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:02.315
that emotion is through

00:03:02.315 --> 00:03:03.650
a filmmaking

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:06.486
technique called performance capture.

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:08.721
And let's take a look at some of that.

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:11.491
It's fascinating You are very hard on

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:16.095
I'm the father.

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:17.564
It's my job.

00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:21.000
What is this stunt story?

00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:24.571
Oh, yes.

00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:27.840
I'll be nice once and then I won't.

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:30.810
I know one thing

00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:35.682
this family is our fortress.

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:40.320
How much harder is

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.155
that, Jim, than straight

00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:45.391
CGI Computer-generated imagery?

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:47.961
And is it this performance capture?

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:48.294
Is it

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:51.030
worth all of the extra time and expense?

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.099
Well, that's really the big question.

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:54.100
I mean, I can talk for hours

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.802
about how we do it,

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:58.037
but we rarely talk about why we do it.

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:00.006
One of the big upsides of it

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:03.543
is that I take all of my cinematography

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:06.179
ideas and I postpone them

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:08.081
into a kind of a post-production.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.415
So what I'm doing with the actors

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:10.650
is not photography,

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:11.884
it's not cinematography.

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:13.152
We're not doing dolly shots

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:14.821
and crane shots and all that.

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:16.356
I'm just working with the actors.

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:18.358
It's a very pure art form.

00:04:18.358 --> 00:04:20.360
It's almost like a theatrical,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.095
like a rehearsal for a stage play.

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:24.564
So as a

00:04:24.564 --> 00:04:26.566
as a director, I'm not distracted

00:04:26.566 --> 00:04:27.767
by all that other stuff.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:29.669
I'm just focusing on character.

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:31.137
So I quite like it.

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:32.071
I'm not sure that it's

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:34.274
necessarily justified if you can.

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:35.575
If you could film something

00:04:35.575 --> 00:04:37.644
with actors, by all means film it.

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:39.512
But I can't create these characters

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:40.179
any other way.

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.248
I can't create this world any other way.

00:04:42.248 --> 00:04:44.284
And there's something about that

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:45.885
dreamlike experience

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:46.819
that people are having

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:47.453
where they're dreaming

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:47.987
with their eyes

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:48.488
wide open

00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:49.922
and it looks completely real,

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:51.758
but it can't possibly be.

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:51.891
That,

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:53.893
I think, is a big part of the appeal of

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.461
these films.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:58.831
Now, when Sam Worthington and Zoe

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:01.668
Saldana are doing a scene together

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:02.935
and you've got this camera

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:04.771
right in their face,

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:07.040
are they doing the scene together

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.242
or is each one do? Absolutely.

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:11.077
We work with everybody together

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:12.845
as much as possible, and that's critical

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:14.147
because when there's no set there

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:15.615
and there's no world there.

00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:17.250
It's all about the actors

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:18.685
looking in each other's eyes

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:20.920
and getting that sense of that truth

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.156
and that emotional reality in the scene.

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:25.258
So we can capture up to 20 people

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:26.993
at a time, which means we can do

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:28.461
crowd scenes, we can do group

00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:30.063
ensemble type scenes, we can do

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:32.165
one on one scenes like the ones that

00:05:32.165 --> 00:05:33.232
you were looking at there.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:35.435
So the

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:36.669
important the critical thing is

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:39.205
the actors have each other to play too.

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:40.440
And and that's

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.174
what allows them

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:43.176
to get to that deep truth of the scene.

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:46.879
Even, I think more than the first avatar,

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.850
you really portray the conflict

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.552
between nature

00:05:52.552 --> 00:05:53.286
and Mann's

00:05:53.286 --> 00:05:56.322
exploitation of natural resources.

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:59.392
And here's some of that

00:06:01.394 --> 00:06:04.030
Protect the people

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:11.571
Let's get it done.

00:06:11.637 --> 00:06:13.239
Your message?

00:06:13.239 --> 00:06:16.175
Maybe it seems to me is even more dire

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:18.144
than it was in the first Avatar

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:19.679
13 years ago.

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:20.480
Yeah, well, look,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:21.414
I mean, the world's not.

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:23.116
The natural world is not getting better.

00:06:23.116 --> 00:06:23.983
It's suffering more.

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:24.917
And more every day

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:26.719
in terms of species extinction.

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:28.921
And we're really focusing

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:31.657
with this film on the ocean

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:33.793
and the creatures of the ocean.

00:06:33.793 --> 00:06:35.094
The habitat of the ocean.

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.028
And it's not doing that

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:37.797
well on our on our world.

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:39.332
And we have to do better.

00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:39.632
I mean,

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:41.601
I don't think the film is preachy,

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:43.436
but the message is clear

00:06:43.436 --> 00:06:45.505
and maybe if people enjoy the film,

00:06:45.505 --> 00:06:46.372
they go on the ride,

00:06:46.372 --> 00:06:47.340
they enjoy the action

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.341
and the emotion of the movie,

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:49.008
but they come out

00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:50.143
thinking, hmm,

00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:52.779
maybe, maybe we do need to be less

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:54.280
destructive and more connected

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.948
to the natural world.

00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:57.016
Avatar two

00:06:57.016 --> 00:06:57.884
has grossed

00:06:57.884 --> 00:06:59.185
according to the last numbers

00:06:59.185 --> 00:07:03.022
I saw, about $1.4 billion.

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:05.158
It is an enormous hit.

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:07.693
But you have said you've set the marker

00:07:07.994 --> 00:07:12.131
that it needs to make at least $2 billion

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:15.468
to break even to justify another one.

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:17.403
And that that, I guess, is

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:19.105
is the question. I have one.

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:20.339
Are you going to make that figure

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:23.409
of $2 billion and two

00:07:23.409 --> 00:07:24.143
if you don't?

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:24.811
Does that mean

00:07:24.811 --> 00:07:26.612
we're not going to see Avatar three?

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:27.747
It looks like

00:07:27.747 --> 00:07:28.748
just with the momentum

00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:29.782
that the film has now,

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:30.483
that will easily

00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:31.851
pass or break

00:07:31.851 --> 00:07:34.353
even in the next few days, actually.

00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:34.987
So.

00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:36.589
So it looks like

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:38.024
I can't wiggle out of this.

00:07:38.024 --> 00:07:39.725
I'm going to have to do these other

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:40.760
these other sequels.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:41.561
So I kind of know

00:07:41.561 --> 00:07:42.328
what I'm going to be doing

00:07:42.328 --> 00:07:43.996
for the next six or seven years.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:47.033
And I'm sure that we'll have a discussion

00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:49.469
soon, you know, with with the top folks

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:50.670
at Disney about,

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.305
you know, the game plan going forward.

00:07:52.305 --> 00:07:53.906
For Avatar three,

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:55.141
which is already in the can.

00:07:55.141 --> 00:07:56.209
We've already

00:07:56.409 --> 00:07:58.644
captured and photographed the whole film

00:07:59.445 --> 00:08:00.580
and then Avatar four

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:01.347
and five or

00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:02.081
are both written

00:08:02.081 --> 00:08:04.650
we even have some A4 in the can.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:05.852
So, you know,

00:08:05.852 --> 00:08:08.421
I think we can see that that

00:08:08.421 --> 00:08:09.422
I think we've begun

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:10.790
a franchise at this point.

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:12.558
How crushing

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:13.726
and I hope

00:08:13.726 --> 00:08:15.828
and you think it's not going to happen,

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:17.296
but how crushing would it be to

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:20.333
you personally if you didn't get to tell

00:08:20.566 --> 00:08:23.336
the entire arc of the Avatar story?

00:08:24.203 --> 00:08:25.204
Not so bad.

00:08:25.204 --> 00:08:25.771
I mean, look,

00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:27.073
I understand this business

00:08:27.073 --> 00:08:28.674
and I understand the vagaries of it

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:29.842
and the variables.

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:31.811
And, you know, the old expression

00:08:31.811 --> 00:08:33.980
you know, man proposes and God disposes.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:35.281
You never quite know

00:08:35.281 --> 00:08:36.482
what's going to happen.

00:08:36.482 --> 00:08:37.950
I also believe in planning

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:38.851
for the upside,

00:08:38.851 --> 00:08:41.387
not not sort of just making a movie

00:08:41.621 --> 00:08:42.421
herky jerky

00:08:42.421 --> 00:08:43.890
and then waiting to see what happens,

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:46.025
but plan for the upside and then accept,

00:08:46.025 --> 00:08:47.560
except that if it doesn't work.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:48.361
I read somewhere

00:08:48.361 --> 00:08:49.829
recently that you said,

00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:50.630
and let me get it right,

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:52.798
I don't want to do anything

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:55.001
but big swings.

00:08:55.001 --> 00:08:58.070
What if at some point between now

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.372
and whenever

00:08:59.372 --> 00:09:01.107
the economics of the business

00:09:01.107 --> 00:09:02.408
were to be such that,

00:09:02.408 --> 00:09:02.909
as you say,

00:09:02.909 --> 00:09:03.609
we're at 60

00:09:03.609 --> 00:09:06.879
or 70% of the pre-pandemic movie going?

00:09:07.079 --> 00:09:08.648
If the economics of the industry

00:09:08.648 --> 00:09:10.116
just becomes such

00:09:10.116 --> 00:09:13.619
that it doesn't allow for big swings like

00:09:14.186 --> 00:09:16.756
Terminator and Titanic and Avatar,

00:09:17.557 --> 00:09:18.324
I think it's fine.

00:09:18.324 --> 00:09:19.825
I mean, I'm a storyteller.

00:09:19.825 --> 00:09:22.862
I love having a camera on my shoulder.

00:09:22.862 --> 00:09:23.930
I love hand-holding.

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:25.498
I love working with actors.

00:09:25.498 --> 00:09:26.332
You know,

00:09:26.332 --> 00:09:28.134
I'll still be able to get a job

00:09:28.134 --> 00:09:29.101
as a storyteller.

00:09:29.101 --> 00:09:30.903
People still need their stories

00:09:30.903 --> 00:09:31.904
and whether that's

00:09:31.904 --> 00:09:32.972
whether they're being produced

00:09:32.972 --> 00:09:35.474
strictly for television, for streaming,

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:38.744
I can live with that as long as everybody

00:09:38.744 --> 00:09:39.745
else has to live with it.

